[38;2;124;124;124mSo $P^2 = P$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 4: $P$ is unbounded.** We have $\|P(u_n)\| = \|f(u_n) \cdot x_0\| = n \|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mx_0\|$, while $\|u_n\| = 1$. So $\|P(u_n)\|/\|u_n\| = n \|x_0\| \to \infty$, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproving $P$ is unbounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, $P$ is an unbounded operator on $X$ with $\text{Dom}(P) = X$ and $P^2[0m[38;2;124;124;124m= P$. $\blacksquare$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is clean and correct. Let me also note that this uses the Axiom of Choice [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(for the Hamel basis), which is standard in functional analysis.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also double check: is there any subtlety about "unbounded operator"? In [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunctional analysis, an "unbounded operator" typically means a linear operator [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthat is not bounded (not continuous). Some definitions require it to be densely [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdefined or closed, but the problem just says "unbounded operator $P$ on some [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mBanach space $X$ such that $\text{Dom}(P) = X$ and $P^2 = P$". The condition $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\text{Dom}(P) = X$ is explicitly given, so "unbounded" just means not bounded. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mOur $P$ fits.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, one might worry: is $P$ "closed"? If $P$ were closed and everywhere [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdefined, by the closed graph theorem it would be bounded. So $P$ is not closed. [0m[38;2;124;124;124mThat's fine — the problem doesn't require $P$ to be closed.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe proof is complete. Let me write it up nicely.[0m

